    description: "ADR-0023 §5 reserves the *dispatch family* for a per-family audit before producer-side migration (Phase 3). Currently the dispatch-family values are sitting at pending_classification on the canonical axis (or absent from the registry entirely, surfaced by the comprehensive sweep at PR #3464 close-out). This item is the audit.\n\nCONTEXT (for a dev picking this up cold):\n\nADR-0023 (docs/adr/0023-edge-type-relationship-not-endpoints.md, currently Draft) classifies edge_type values on the relationship / endpoint_shape / pending_classification axis. ADR-0023 §5 explicitly defers the dispatch and publish families to per-family audits because the family members are NOT obviously a single relationship under different protocols — some may be genuinely distinct, others protocol-conditional aliases. From ADR-0023 §5 (transcribed):\n\n> **Dispatch family:** dispatches_to, routes_to, delegates_to,\n>   ...\n>   Some of these are genuinely different relationships; some are\n>   protocol-conditional labels of the same relationship. A case-by-case\n>   audit during migration: some may be genuinely distinct relationships,\n>   others ...\n\nDISPATCH-FAMILY MEMBERS (registered values currently at pending_classification, plus emit-site values surfaced by WI-tavas-voror's sweep that likely belong here):\n\n- dispatches_to (registry, pending_classification) — generic dispatch-through-indirection\n- routes_to (registry, pending_classification) — HTTP/router route to handler\n- delegates_to (sweep, unregistered) — explicit delegation; possibly a synonym of dispatches_to\n- message_dispatch (sweep, unregistered) — dispatch via message-passing\n- annotated_dispatches (sweep, unregistered) — dispatch driven by annotations / decorators\n- uses_dispatch_table (sweep, unregistered) — code uses a dispatch table for routing\n- di_registers (sweep, unregistered) — code registers a binding with a DI container\n- di_resolves (registry, pending_classification) — DI container resolved a binding\n- annotated_publishes (sweep, unregistered) — borderline; could be publish-family — defer to that audit\n\nNote: di_registers and di_resolves are paired (register / resolve); the audit should handle them together, deciding whether one is the canonical and the other folds, or both are distinct relationships.\n\nTHE AUDIT QUESTIONS (per ADR-0023's Decision):\n\nFor each family member, decide:\n\n1. CANONICAL — names a genuinely distinct relationship. Promote to the relationship axis with a clear axiom-conformant description (the value names the relationship the edge expresses between src and dst, not endpoint-shape leakage).\n\n2. FOLD — protocol-conditional or framework-specific alias of an existing canonical. Deprecate the family-specific name; rename to existing canonical (likely 'dispatches_to' or 'calls') plus edge.meta carrying the framework / protocol / mechanism.\n\n3. DEPRECATE-NO-FOLD — the value's meaning is not actually a relationship at all (e.g., it names a structural pattern that should be expressed as a Symbol property rather than an Edge type). Remove from registry; document the migration to a different field if applicable.\n\nApply the four leakage tests from the audit playbook (Step 3 — property derivability, apex/peer overloading, construct vs. relationship, mechanism vs. category). Each verdict requires a written rationale (what query each value answers; why it does or does not collapse with siblings).\n\nDELIVERABLES:\n\n1. Per-value verdict table in this item's discussion thread (audit-style — one row per family member).\n2. ADR amendment or per-family ADR-addendum capturing the verdicts (sibling pattern: ADR-0024 for axis declarations; this is the dispatch-family analogue).\n3. Registry updates: pending_classification → relationship (for canonicals), endpoint_shape (for fold-deprecations), or removal (for deprecate-no-fold).\n4. Update WI-tavas-voror's discussion thread to reflect any sweep-surfaced family members that the audit resolved.\n\nDEPENDENCIES (HARD):\n- ADR-0023 advancement to Proposed/Accepted is desirable so the §5 framing is settled before audits commit verdicts to the registry. If the ADR remains Draft, audit verdicts are themselves Draft until the parent advances.\n\nDEPENDENCIES (SOFT):\n- WI-tavas-voror-rakiv-litog-gatad-zonoz-holah-tugil — overlaps on the sweep-surfaced family members. The audit consumes the sweep's findings; coordinate so each value is classified once.\n- Phase 3 (WI-mokam-jalig-fuhab-gifug-sigij-gotar-sisig-zufoz) — the dispatch-family producer sites cannot be migrated until this audit picks canonical names for the renames.\n\nACCEPTANCE:\n- Every dispatch-family member listed above has a documented verdict (canonical / fold / deprecate) with rationale.\n- The registry reflects the verdicts.\n- An ADR amendment or addendum captures the verdicts as durable governance.\n- The next sweep (e.g., Phase 3 producer migration starting on this family) inherits the canonical landings.\n\nSIBLINGS:\n- Publish-family audit (separate item, file next per the audit-lifecycle session ordering) — same audit shape, different value set.\n- WI-sahab-fatoz, WI-mokam-jalig, WI-vomoj-suhaz — Phase 2/3/4. Phase 3's dispatch-family-touching subset waits on this audit.\n- WI-tavas-voror — 45-value triage. This audit resolves the dispatch-family subset (~8 values) of those 45.\n\nBACKGROUND: surfaced as part of loose-end #9 in the audit-lifecycle session (Apr 2026). ADR-0023 §5 explicitly deferred these family audits; tracker captures the work."  # 0e83
